#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wiki-plain — the READER'S DIGEST layer: one plain-language twin per page.

WHAT THIS IS. Every page in `wiki/` is written for a reader who already knows
the corpus: typed edges, register tables, falsifier lists, provenance
disclosures, `knowledge: earned` and the rest. That is the correct register for
the technical wiki and it is unreadable to somebody who walked in off the
street. So each page may carry a twin under `plain/` — the same finding, at the
same altitude, told to a person who has never read anything else here.

    wiki/mind/synthesis/fayette-return.md   the entry
    plain/mind/synthesis/fayette-return.md  the same entry, in plain English

The portal serves the pair as two MODES behind one switch. `plain/` is not a
second wiki and its files are not pages: no typed edges, no `synthesizes:`, no
Gaps section, no page in the index, nothing to cite. A `plain/` file is a
RENDERING of a page and has no standing of its own.

WHY `plain/` AND NOT `digest/`. `bin/wiki-digest` and `DIGEST.md` already exist
and mean something completely different — the generated ledger of recent
activity. A second thing called "digest" in the same repository is a thing a
future session will conflate with the first, and `bin/wiki-digest` WRITES
files. The reader-facing name is "Reader's Digest" because that is what the
operator asked for; the path is `plain/` so nothing here can be mistaken for
the ledger.

WHY IT IS OUTSIDE `wiki/`. `bin/wiki-lint` globs `wiki/**/*.md` and every page
it finds is a page: it wants frontmatter, typed edges, an index entry, and it
counts toward the master index. A twin living in `wiki/` would double the page
count, orphan-warn forever, and — worst — become citable, so a later synthesis
could reason from a simplification instead of from the finding. Outside `wiki/`
it cannot.

---- THE ONE RULE THAT MATTERS -----------------------------------------------

**A translation records the version it was made from, and goes stale when that
version moves.** `source_modified:` in a `plain/` file is the `date_modified:`
of the page it was written against. When the page is revised, the twin is
stale, `check` says so, and it stays said until somebody re-reads the page and
re-translates it.

This is the same discipline as `bin/wiki-climb check` and it exists for the
same reason. A simplification that silently lags its source is worse than no
simplification: it is a confident, readable, WRONG account of what the wiki
says, served to exactly the reader least equipped to catch it. Never clear a
stale twin by bumping `source_modified:` — re-read the page, decide whether the
plain version survives the change, rewrite what moved.

---- THE MORATORIUM IS ENFORCED HERE, NOT REMEMBERED -------------------------

`CLAUDE.md` carries a standing operator directive: no new narrative about Annie
enters this repository. A plain-language retelling of an Annie page is new
narrative about Annie — it adds no facts, but it is new prose about a living
person in a situation the operator has called dangerous, written to be read by
people who could not read the original. That is squarely inside what the
directive stops.

So it is not left to a session to remember. It is TWO mechanical rules, and the
second is the one that actually guarantees the outcome:

  1. A page SUBSTANTIALLY about her gets no twin at all. `check` fails if one
     exists; `new` refuses; `next` never proposes it. "Substantially" is
     mechanical: named anywhere in the frontmatter (title, aliases, tags,
     sources, `synthesizes:`, a typed-edge target or claim), or more than
     `INCIDENTAL` mentions in the body.
  2. **No `plain/` file may mention her at all, ever, whatever its source.**
     `check` fails on any twin that does. This is the guarantee: rule 1 draws
     a line somebody could argue about, and rule 2 means no new prose about
     her can enter this layer regardless of where the line fell.

Rule 1 is not merely rule 2 applied earlier. A page can be *about* her while
mentioning her a handful of times, and translating one by quietly eliding her
would produce a twin that passes rule 2 and MISREPRESENTS THE PAGE — a reader
told a version of a finding with its subject removed has been misled more
efficiently than by any error. So: a page with an incidental mention is
eligible, and the twin simply does not carry that sentence. If leaving it out
would change what the page says, the page is not translatable — decline it, and
say so where the next session will read it. That judgment belongs to a reader;
the tool only holds the floor under it.

Lifting any of this is the operator's call and nobody else's — it is one
regex and one integer, `MORATORIUM` and `INCIDENTAL`, directly below.

Missing twins are NOT errors. Coverage is standing campaign work, worked
top-down like every other queue in this repository, and a page with no plain
version simply serves the technical one with a note saying so. Only a BROKEN
twin — stale, orphaned, or forbidden — is a gate failure.

    bin/wiki-plain status          # coverage, by domain, and what is stale
    bin/wiki-plain check           # the gate: 0 = clean, 1 = broken twin
    bin/wiki-plain next [n]        # the n densest pages with no twin yet
    bin/wiki-plain new <slug>      # scaffold a twin, frontmatter filled in
"""
import argparse
import os
import re
import sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
WIKI = os.path.join(ROOT, "wiki")
PLAIN = os.path.join(ROOT, "plain")

# The standing directive in CLAUDE.md, as code. See the two rules in the module
# docstring. Only the operator lifts either of these.
# Leading word boundary only, and no trailing one. `\bannie\b` looks tighter and
# is wrong: it misses `annie_metadata_24h.csv`, the filename this material is
# cited by throughout, because `_` is a word character and kills the boundary.
# read-receipt-forensics carries that filename twice and read as eligible until
# this was fixed. The surname rides along for the same reason — the guard should
# not depend on which of the two a given page happens to use.
MORATORIUM = re.compile(r"\b(annie|ulmer)", re.I)

# How many body mentions a page may carry and still count as incidental rather
# than as being about her. Two: a page that names somebody three times is
# talking about them. The 2026-08-28 distribution over 518 pages — 277 at zero,
# 24 at one, 32 at two, then a long tail — puts the natural break here.
INCIDENTAL = 2

# Archived artifacts are pinned oversized things nobody updates; they are exempt
# from budgets in wiki-lint and they are exempt from translation here too.
SKIP_DIRS = {".obsidian", "archive"}


def read(path):
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        return fh.read()


def frontmatter(text):
    """The shapes these files use — flat `key: value` only. Twins have no lists."""
    if not text.startswith("---"):
        return {}
    end = text.find("\n---", 3)
    if end == -1:
        return {}
    meta = {}
    for line in text[4:end].split("\n"):
        m = re.match(r"^([A-Za-z0-9_]+):\s*(.*)$", line)
        if m and m.group(2).strip():
            meta[m.group(1)] = m.group(2).strip().strip("\"'")
    return meta


def body_of(text):
    """Everything after the frontmatter block."""
    if not text.startswith("---"):
        return text
    end = text.find("\n---", 3)
    if end == -1:
        return text
    nl = text.find("\n", end + 1)
    return text[nl + 1:] if nl != -1 else ""


def words(text):
    return len(body_of(text).split())


def walk(root):
    """Slugs (`domain/path/name`, no extension) under a tree, skipping SKIP_DIRS."""
    out = []
    if not os.path.isdir(root):
        return out
    for dirpath, dirs, files in os.walk(root):
        dirs[:] = [d for d in dirs if d not in SKIP_DIRS and not d.startswith(".")]
        for f in sorted(files):
            if not f.endswith(".md"):
                continue
            rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dirpath, f), root)
            out.append(rel.replace(os.sep, "/")[:-3])
    return sorted(out)


class Page:
    """One wiki page and whatever the Reader's Digest layer has to say about it."""

    def __init__(self, slug):
        self.slug = slug
        self.src_path = os.path.join(WIKI, *slug.split("/")) + ".md"
        self.twin_path = os.path.join(PLAIN, *slug.split("/")) + ".md"
        src = read(self.src_path)
        self.meta = frontmatter(src)
        self.domain = slug.split("/")[0]
        self.page_type = self.meta.get("page_type", "")
        self.modified = self.meta.get("date_modified", "")
        self.words = words(src)

        # MORATORIUM RULE 1, judged on the source text rather than on the slug:
        # a page can be titled for somebody else entirely and still be carrying
        # her narrative. Frontmatter counts at any density — a name in
        # `synthesizes:`, in a typed-edge claim or in `sources:` means the page
        # is built on that material however sparingly the body says so.
        head = src[: src.find("\n---", 3)] if src.startswith("---") else ""
        self.excluded = bool(MORATORIUM.search(head)) or (
            len(MORATORIUM.findall(body_of(src))) > INCIDENTAL
        )

        self.twin = os.path.exists(self.twin_path)
        twin_src = read(self.twin_path) if self.twin else ""
        self.twin_meta = frontmatter(twin_src) if self.twin else {}
        self.twin_words = words(twin_src) if self.twin else 0
        # MORATORIUM RULE 2 — the guarantee. Nothing in this layer names her.
        self.twin_names_her = bool(MORATORIUM.search(twin_src)) if self.twin else False

    @property
    def stale(self):
        """The page moved after the twin was written against it."""
        if not self.twin:
            return False
        against = self.twin_meta.get("source_modified", "")
        return bool(self.modified) and against != self.modified

    @property
    def ratio(self):
        return (self.twin_words / self.words) if self.words and self.twin else 0.0


def load():
    return [Page(s) for s in walk(WIKI)]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def cmd_check(_args):
    """The gate. Broken twins only — a missing twin is work, not a defect."""
    pages = load()
    by_slug = {p.slug: p for p in pages}
    errors = []

    for p in pages:
        # Rule 2 first: it is the guarantee, and it holds even where rule 1
        # decided the source page was fine to work from.
        if p.twin_names_her:
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: plain/{p.slug}.md names a person under the standing "
                f"moratorium in CLAUDE.md. No file in this layer may, whatever "
                f"its source page says. Remove the passage — and if removing it "
                f"changes what the page means, delete the twin instead and "
                f"record the page as one that cannot be translated."
            )
        if p.twin and p.excluded:
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: has a plain/ twin but the page is under the standing "
                f"moratorium in CLAUDE.md. Delete plain/{p.slug}.md — a plain-"
                f"language retelling is new narrative, and only the operator "
                f"lifts this."
            )
        if p.stale:
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: twin is STALE — written against "
                f"{p.twin_meta.get('source_modified') or '(nothing)'}, page is now "
                f"{p.modified}. Re-read the page and re-translate what moved. "
                f"Never just bump source_modified:."
            )
        if p.twin and p.twin_meta.get("plain_of") != f"wiki/{p.slug}":
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: twin's plain_of: is "
                f"'{p.twin_meta.get('plain_of', '(missing)')}', expected "
                f"'wiki/{p.slug}'."
            )

    # A twin with no page above it: the page was renamed or deleted and the
    # translation was left behind pointing at nothing.
    for slug in walk(PLAIN):
        if slug not in by_slug:
            errors.append(
                f"{slug}: plain/{slug}.md is an ORPHAN — no wiki/{slug}.md above "
                f"it. The page was renamed or removed; move or delete the twin."
            )

    for e in errors:
        print(f"  ERROR  {e}")
    covered = sum(1 for p in pages if p.twin)
    eligible = sum(1 for p in pages if not p.excluded)
    print(
        f"\nwiki-plain: {len(errors)} error(s) · {covered}/{eligible} eligible "
        f"pages have a Reader's Digest twin"
    )
    return 1 if errors else 0


def cmd_status(args):
    pages = load()
    eligible = [p for p in pages if not p.excluded]
    covered = [p for p in pages if p.twin]
    stale = [p for p in pages if p.stale]

    print("READER'S DIGEST COVERAGE\n")
    print(f"  pages in wiki/          {len(pages)}")
    print(f"  eligible                {len(eligible)}")
    print(f"  withheld (moratorium)   {len(pages) - len(eligible)}")
    print(f"  translated              {len(covered)}")
    print(f"  stale                   {len(stale)}")
    if eligible:
        print(f"  coverage                {100 * len(covered) / len(eligible):.1f}%")

    print("\n  BY DOMAIN")
    for d in sorted({p.domain for p in pages}):
        rows = [p for p in pages if p.domain == d]
        el = [p for p in rows if not p.excluded]
        cv = [p for p in rows if p.twin]
        print(f"    {d:<12} {len(cv):>3}/{len(el):<4} translated   ({len(rows)} pages)")

    if covered:
        print("\n  TRANSLATED")
        for p in sorted(covered, key=lambda p: p.slug):
            flag = "  ** STALE **" if p.stale else ""
            print(
                f"    {p.slug:<48} {p.words:>6} -> {p.twin_words:<6} "
                f"({p.ratio * 100:.0f}%){flag}"
            )

    if stale:
        print("\n  STALE — re-read the page, then re-translate what moved:")
        for p in stale:
            print(
                f"    {p.slug}  (twin written against "
                f"{p.twin_meta.get('source_modified') or '(nothing)'}, "
                f"page now {p.modified})"
            )
    return 0


def cmd_next(args):
    """Densest eligible pages with no twin — the queue, worked top-down."""
    todo = [p for p in load() if not p.twin and not p.excluded]
    todo.sort(key=lambda p: -p.words)
    if args.synthesis:
        todo = [p for p in todo if p.page_type == "synthesis"]
    if not todo:
        print("Nothing eligible is untranslated.")
        return 0
    print(f"NEXT {min(args.n, len(todo))} — densest first, {len(todo)} outstanding\n")
    for p in todo[: args.n]:
        kind = p.page_type or "-"
        print(f"  {p.words:>6}w  {kind:<12} {p.slug}")
    print("\n  bin/wiki-plain new <slug>   to scaffold one")
    return 0


SCAFFOLD = """---
plain_of: wiki/{slug}
title: "{title}"
reading_level: general
date_modified: {today}
source_modified: {source_modified}
---

# {title}

**The short version.** <One or two sentences. What would you tell somebody in
an elevator? No jargon, no citations, no hedging — the finding itself.>

## <A plain heading, phrased as what a reader would ask>

<Write it the way you would explain it to a smart person who has never read
this wiki. Keep every fact and every number. Drop the apparatus: typed edges,
register tables, provenance disclosures and `knowledge:` values are machinery
for maintaining the wiki, not part of the finding.>

## What would prove this wrong

<The technical page's falsifiers, in plain words. Keep them. A reader who is
only shown the confident half is being sold something.>

## What we still don't know

<The gaps, in plain words. Same reason.>
"""


def cmd_new(args):
    slug = args.slug.removeprefix("wiki/").removesuffix(".md")
    src = os.path.join(WIKI, *slug.split("/")) + ".md"
    if not os.path.exists(src):
        print(f"No such page: wiki/{slug}.md", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    page = Page(slug)
    if page.excluded:
        print(
            f"REFUSED: wiki/{slug}.md is under the standing moratorium in "
            f"CLAUDE.md.\nA plain-language retelling is new narrative about a "
            f"living person. Only the operator lifts this.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 2
    if page.twin:
        print(f"plain/{slug}.md already exists.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    import datetime

    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(page.twin_path), exist_ok=True)
    with open(page.twin_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(
            SCAFFOLD.format(
                slug=slug,
                title=page.meta.get("title", slug.split("/")[-1]).strip("\"'"),
                today=datetime.date.today().isoformat(),
                source_modified=page.modified,
            )
        )
    print(f"Wrote plain/{slug}.md  (source is {page.words} words)")
    print("Read the whole page before you translate it. Keep every number.")
    return 0


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split("\n")[0])
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd")

    sub.add_parser("check", help="gate: broken twins only")
    sub.add_parser("status", help="coverage report")

    n = sub.add_parser("next", help="densest untranslated pages")
    n.add_argument("n", nargs="?", type=int, default=10)
    n.add_argument("--synthesis", action="store_true", help="synthesis pages only")

    w = sub.add_parser("new", help="scaffold a twin")
    w.add_argument("slug")

    args = ap.parse_args()
    return {
        "check": cmd_check,
        "status": cmd_status,
        "next": cmd_next,
        "new": cmd_new,
        None: cmd_status,
    }[args.cmd](args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    # `status` is long and gets piped to `head` constantly. Without this that is
    # a BrokenPipeError traceback instead of output.
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except BrokenPipeError:
        os.dup2(os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY), sys.stdout.fileno())
        sys.exit(0)
